-- title:
--   Admissible rigidified modules admit a Cartier quadruple
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota \colon W(\mathbb F_{p^2}) \to W(k)$; write $\bar\jmath$ for $\iota$ followed by reduction modulo the ideal generated by $p$. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $W(k)/pW(k)$ (a commutative two-dimensional formal group law with an action of $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi^2 = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [\sigma a] \circ \varpi$) which is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. $\mathrm{Lie}(\Phi)_0$ and $\mathrm{Lie}(\Phi)_1$ are complementary invertible submodules, and whose $[p]$-kernel has degree $p^4$; assume $\varpi$ annihilates $\mathrm{Lie}(\Phi)_0$, that the two Teichmüller-graded pieces of degree $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ are complementary (hypothesis $h_{c\Phi}$, entering the associated graded Cartier module data $D_\Phi$), and let $r_\Phi \colon \mathbb Z_p^2 \to N(D_\Phi)$ be an additive map such that $D_\Phi$ admits at least one canonical $L$-map and, for every canonical $L$-map $L$, $r_\Phi$ is a bijection of $\mathbb Z_p^2$ onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece $\eta(L) \cap N(D_\Phi)_0$. Let $B$ be a Noetherian $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra in which $p$ is nilpotent, let $\psi \colon W(k) \to B$ be a ring homomorphism, and let $t = (X, n, \rho)$ be a rigidified object over $B$ which is admissible for $(\iota,\psi)$: $X$ is special for $\psi \circ \iota$, has height $4$, and $\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4n$ from the base change $\Phi_{\psi}$ to $X \bmod p$. Then there exists a Drinfeld datum $Q$ over $B$ for $(\mathbb Z_p \subset \mathbb Q_p, \pi = p)$ — families of full $\mathbb Z_p$-lattices $N_0(x) \subseteq N_1(x)$ in $\mathbb Q_p^2$ indexed by $x \in \operatorname{Spec} B$ with $pN_1(x) \subseteq N_0(x)$ and open membership conditions, invertible $B$-modules $T_0, T_1$ with maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to multiplication by $p$, and stalkwise comparison maps $u_0, u_1$ — which is a Cartier quadruple for $t$: $\rho$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules, there are $B$-linear isomorphisms $T_0 \cong \mathrm{Lie}(X)_0$ and $T_1 \cong \mathrm{Lie}(X)_1$ carrying $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ to the action of $\varpi$ on $\mathrm{Lie}(X)$, and at every prime $x$ the lattice $N_i(x)$ consists exactly of those $v \in \mathbb Q_p^2$ admitting, over some localisation $B[1/f]$ with $f \notin x$, an $\eta$-section of index $i$ representing $v$, together with the remaining compatibilities between $u_0, u_1$ and the germs of these sections (summarised here).
--
--   This is the existence half of the Čerednik–Drinfeld dictionary in the form used by Boutot and Carayol: it attaches to an admissible rigidified special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over a $p$-nilpotent Noetherian base the lattice-theoretic datum that describes a point of Drinfeld's formal model of the $p$-adic upper half plane. It is invoked in the identification of the deformation functor with that formal model, in particular in the statements describing how the datum behaves on the strata and along edge isogenies.
